      "writtenOn": "2024-12-14T21:14:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s a good point, it should be consistent across backends.\n\nFair that it\u0027s unlikely we would actually ever run into the problematic case where the backend doesn\u0027t support texture views but does have multiplanar textures.\n\nAnd that\u0027s probably good enough, I guess it doesn\u0027t really matter since it\u0027s an internal API for now and not standardized. My brain is always in standardization mode. :)\n\n---\n\nThat said I\u0027m just wondering, do both of these checks need to be skipped for multiplanar textures? Or only the second one? If only the second one then it would be nice to move the condition to wrap only that one. Mostly because it\u0027ll be a bit more self-documenting; the first validation check isn\u0027t that important even if it is valid.\n\nBut if that doesn\u0027t make sense then never mind.",
      "parentUuid": "73adc3d7_095dd7f0",
      "revId": "e9529004c6e48900ebfe1eacf18b2edc9dfdcd05",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d57328db_7dd47081",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/BindGroup.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 211,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-14T22:16:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We need to skip both because on Windows, hardware encoder uses a 2D array texture containing multiple frames. And to view a frame we need to create multiple views that each:\n1. Views a layer in the array texture. i.e. dimension \u003d `TextureViewDimension::e2D`\n2. points to a plane in the layer. i.e. aspect \u003d `TextureAspect::Plane*`.\n\nBoth of the above are disabllowed by 2 compat mode\u0027s checks. So we want to skip both for the multiplanar textures.\n\nIf it\u0027s a bit confusing. Maybe we can add a new toggle `AllowTexureViewsInCompatMode` that basically skips the texture views restrictions altogether from the compat mode. This toggle is only meant for internal chrome\u0027s code to use. So for example, in chrome\u0027s code if we detect that we are on windows, we can turn on this toggle. WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "67744286_8e2281db",
      "revId": "e9529004c6e48900ebfe1eacf18b2edc9dfdcd05",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
